e t cells are to begin with just  overall to do this we'll look at cd3  c4 cb8 so we can see c3  showing up very clearly in the top  clusters there  which you know matches up with you know  cb4 and cba in different locations which  looks good  however you'll see right off the bat  actually cd4  is showing an interesting difference  from the rest and in fact what it turns  out is that  cd4 is expressed also in myeloid cells  which is that  large cluster near the bottom so this is  a  really clear example of a case where you  know i think most people think of c4 as  the t marker of cb4-t cells but  on the rna level at least it's expressed  quite highly in myeloid cells as well so  it cannot be used  reliably alone as a marker in that case  so once we have identified our t cells  usually what we'll do is we'll pull them  out and we'll recluster them  and the idea there is that by  re-clustering you know within just that  cell type  sometimes this helps pull apart subtypes  better because we're basically trying to  cluster just  on the variation within the t cells it  doesn't always work  but sometimes it helps a little bit so  we'll do that  and so this new plot that i'm showing is  now just the t  cells and now we'll try to identify some  subtypes  so first we'll look at t-regs t-regs are  generally  um on the easier side to identify  so i'll look here at cd4 fox p3  il-2ra which is cd25 and ctla4  so you can see although none of these  genes is completely specific  they're all kind of pointing us in the  same direction of this small cluster  near the bottom that i've circled which  are most likely to be our t-rex  so because we have information from  multiple genes we can kind of build up  confidence that that's  probably most likely the t-rex but i  think it's really  interesting to note you can see here how  you know none of these  is completely specific even box p3 um  you know there are a lot of possible  explanations for why you see expression  elsewhere  but in any case you can just see that  basically you can't just take it alone  as the ultimate predictor of t-regs and  in addition you can see  if you zoom in on the cluster you can  see not all the cells  are even expressing fox p3 so you know  it's  this is why we usually take this  clustering approach because basically  you know we can't just gate on fox v3  because you're going to have a lot of  cells that where you're not detecting it  and so  what you do is you rely on the cluster  information where we say oh well  enrichment of cells here express boxy3  therefore we're going to infer that the  rest of them probably express it as well  and that they're probably all t-regs  in any case so you know it's although  there is some  ambiguity overall this is a fairly  confident  identification of subtype  okay so then another subtype we might  want to try to identify is  th2 this is a type of t helper cell we  can look at cd4  gata3 ccr4 il4 which are you know  typical markers you might find in the  literature  all right well i already showed you guys  three which is not specific and  therefore  is not helpful here dcr4 looks a little  bit more  promising however ccr4 is actually  expressed in other subtypes of cd4 t  cells and so we can't  use it alone as a as a definitive  definitive marker h2  and then il4 has the unfortunate case of  just not being detected widely  and you know we really can't do cell  type identification based on just one  cell so unfortunately this was a failure  to identify the subtype  finally one last example i'll show is  you know trying to make this overall  broad distinction between naive effector  central memory and effector memory  t cells and so usually the way we'll do  this is  look at the combination of c45 ra  positive versus ro positive  and then cb 62l positive and negative  cells and so if we look at these genes  on the rna level  we're showing what they look like on the  bottom the cd45 you know ra and ro are  basically isoforms cd45 and  unfortunately  we just don't have that information on  the rna bubble in this case  cd62l you know maybe has a little bit of  signal here but  it's a bit messy and so it's hard to  make a clear call on what's going on  however so fortunately this is actually  a sightseeing data set so we have  information on 29 surface proteins here  which includes cd62l  and cd45ra and ro and so  now by looking at the protein level  information  we can see a much clearer picture about  where  these genes are actually being expressed  on the surface  um and so this allows us to actually  make a  reasonably confident prediction about  what what  these different subtypes where they are  in this cluster  plot um so i think  you know i think sightseek and really  just you know  methods that can bring in this sort of  protein information  are going to be really really helpful  for doing cell subtype identification  going forward and there are even methods  that are being developed now  that that will allow you to combine the  surface protein information with the rna  information at the same time  and do a joint sort of clustering on  both of those pieces of information  which generally results in you know more  clearly defined clusters  which makes the whole process a lot  easier  all right so you might be thinking uh  well what about automated cell type  identification  so i think this is a really exciting  area and something i've been  paying a lot of attention to um i i do  hope that someday this will be  the answer to this question however  at least right now i have yet to see  evidence that  automated annotation is ready for prime  time so  i think the figure that i'm showing here  really illustrates that so this is from  a  recent benchmarking of cell type  automated cell type  identification methods that came out in  2019  one thing i should say is you know this  field is moving very quickly so already  a paper from 2019 is probably quite out  of date  so there may be improvements here  however you know what i want to direct  your attention to  is in this plot so they're showing the  performance of various different methods  on different tests  and you can see if you look at the inter  data set  test and then look at the deep  annotation level so right in the middle  that column you can see across the board  all methods are doing very poorly on  this test so basically in other words  when you try to use one training set  to classify a completely separate  separate test set  that's the enter data set test method  and then try to do this on a deep  annotation level so subtypes  no method is able to handle that right  now again  there may be improvements since this  paper but basically this is where  all the method this is a real pain point  right now  so we've tested several methods in-house  and you know including um svm method  which was the top performer in this  benchmark as well as  the threat transfer anchor approach  which we found also performed  almost identically with svm and so what  we've  basically replica replicated this  finding where basically we when we test  we build the classifier based on pvmcs  and then test on pbmcs  we see good performance but then when we  try to  generalize this to another dataset it's  a bit of a mess  so for example i'm showing here that  same classifier that was trained on  pvmcs but now applied to the human  kidney atlas  dataset so this contains um  so i'm showing here the cd45 positive  cells so you can see that they're  basically a lot of non-immune cells in  this dataset from the kidney  and of course you know our pbmc  classifier doesn't know anything about  non-immune cells so it's not going to be  able to classify them  however we would expect that because we  have a threshold on  probability for classification or  basically anything that isn't high  confidence enough  we mark as unclassified one would hope  that all of this  other non-immune stuff would be  classified would be  marked as unclassified however this is  not the case what we see  is basically these things are all being  classified as t cells and apparently  quite confidently because otherwise they  wouldn't have passed that threshold  so this is you know unfortunate i guess  not maybe not too unexpected because  you know this is a very difficult  problem to try and generalize to a  completely different context  one ray of hope i see is that at least  for the cell types that were in common  between the training and testing  we see reasonably good performance it's  not 100  but it's it's fairly good so i think you  know there's promise here but what we  really need are  you know either methods that can really  do a good job generalizing to new  contexts  or we need better reference data sets so  that we can have  you know the right reference for any new  data set that comes up  i think a combination of those two  things is where we're going to be  heading in the future  so then this question of you know  automation overall  are we there yet i see because single  cell analysis takes so long there's  always this urge to try and automate it  to make it faster  i want to urge caution here you know i  think basically we're at a point where  yes  certain steps can be automated without  too much trouble  but others you really need to be  cautious  like i mentioned you know the initial  steps of data pre-processing  is not really a big issue to automate  we're getting closer for the cell qc and  technical effect adjustment methods  however you know when you get the cell  type identification um i think in most  cases unless you're doing a very  you know um you know even if even if  you're doing pbmc to pbmc type of  automated classification  you still need to be careful and check  those results in a manual way because  it's just really important to get those  things right because you know all of  your downstream analysis depends on it  so you know i i would say this is still  an area where  you know even if you use a manual  automated method to supplement  you still need to do manual review  hopefully we're on our way though  to getting to automation here  okay and so you know just looking  forward um i see  a lot of challenges that we're going to  have to face as a field i already  mentioned automated cell type  identification  another big one i see is large-scale  integration you know a lot  we do have integration methods now  however um  you know the integrated and corrected  data account uh tables that were  produced by this  are not really appropriate for  downstream analyses so we need to think  about ways that we can actually  ask questions across large compendiums  of data  that way we can actually make use of all  the signals of data that's being  generated  and not be limited to just looking at  one study at a time  i'm also really excited about  multi-omics integration but like i  mentioned  you know integrating surface protein  information can be hugely helpful for  cell type identification  and there are many other types of single  cell omics that can be done as well so  single phallic atoxique  so on and so forth and all of these can  be potentially integrated together  to give a better full picture of what's  going on in the cell  we're also going to have to think about  scaling up of course especially if we're  thinking about looking across many  different studies  many different types of omics data  together  there's going to be definite scaling  issues that start occurring  i think you know if we want to make use  of all the data that's going to come out  of human cell atlas these are challenges  we're going to have to start thinking  about  so with that i hope i've given you a  good overview of how single cell rna-seq  can be useful for drug discovery as well  as disease understanding in general  as well as you know some of the  challenges we're currently facing and  you know where  where the field is hopefully moving  towards so  wit