oking at the most highly  expressed genes  and this is a pretty big bias you know  you're and that's only like 2 000 genes  that you're looking at  uh i think to some extent the focusing  on cell types first can  alleviate this a little bit because you  know you you first figure out the cell  type hypothesis  and then you can also look at  on a whole what's going different in  that cell type on the pathway level  and then maybe you can infer well any  gene in this pathway  is probably relevant and so hopefully  you know you can  use that to generalize to more than what  you've actually detected  uh you can you can apply this of course  as well with just doing  direct differential expression between  health and disease um  so i think approaches like that are  important  when thinking about the drug discovery  aspect of this um  but overall i think you know dropouts  and  just missing like you know lower quality  data are things that we just have to  deal with with single cell  on a case-by-case basis because it's  often quite different for every data  dataset  wonderful thank you so much for that so  our next question  what would you say are the most common  bias when interpreting these types of  data  right so um there are a lot of different  biases  that come with single cell rna-seq and i  think we just talked about  um the problem of you know detecting the  most highly expressed genes right so  you have a bias there um another thing  that can come in  with cell types especially so when  thinking about  cell type proportions um you know not  all cell types  are equally well captured in single cell  so some  don't survive well during the  dissociation process if you're working  with let's say a tissue  it depends a lot on the technology  you're using but there's  been many times shown actually with  different tissues  that certain cell types are being kind  of selected against when you do single  cell  and this is potentially very problematic  if you're relying on 